[Music]  what's up guys today we are making curry  puffs and it is a family favorite of  mine and what a curry puff is is a puff  pastry with a curried beef filling in  the middle so it's crispy on the outside  and soft and moist in the middle it's a  wonderful snack to have to bring to a  party a potluck and I remember making  these with my mom when I was a kid for  me it's a very nostalgic thing to make  and this is a very Cantonese kind of dim  sum and it's one of the many things that  my mom taught me over the years and it's  really sweet as my mom actually taught  this to TIFF - so Tiffany knows how to  make this as well so a little back story  on this curry puff it is a traditionally  denison like pastry and yet had a lot of  Cantonese bakeries a lot of Hong Kong  bakeries now within the culinary history  of Hong Kong Hong Kong has had a lot of  influence from other cuisines in the  world Hong Kong has been influenced by  British food Portuguese food Indian food  and because of the Portuguese food  Portugal was influenced a lot by Africa  because of a lot of the trading that  they were used to do back in the day  plus all the colonizing in Hong Kong and  Macau this region in China has had a lot  of European influences and so over the  years the curry puff has now become  pretty standard fare in Hong Kong  cuisine and I'm gonna share that with  you today to begin we are going to start  with the skillet and we're gonna pour  about two to three tablespoons of oil  first we're gonna add in our diced onion  and we're gonna saute that until it get  starts to turn translucent from there  we're gonna add our minced garlic Dante  that for a couple of minutes and let it  release its aroma and sense and once we  have the onion translucent worked in  with the garlic we throw in our one  pound of ground beef and make sure to  work your ground beef in move it around  so it does  stick to the skillet anyone have brown  all the beef and then once the beef is  browned and we have the onions and  garlic all mixed in together from there  we're gonna pour in all the spices that  we have so that's the two tablespoons of  curry powder two teaspoons of turmeric  powder the one teaspoon of cumin powder  the quarter teaspoon of sugar and  quarter teaspoon of black pepper and the  1 and 1/2 teaspoon of salt and once you  start mixing that together you'll start  to notice the turmeric powder and the  curry powder really bringing out this  beautiful yellow color into the curry  making the filling is super super simple  it's just sauteing all these ingredients  together but there are second half of  the cooking process is to bake the  pastries now in the baking process it's  very easy to dry out the filling so in  order to counter that we're gonna add in  cornstarch slurry with some beef broth  and with this we're gonna add to our  sauteed beef now once the corn starch  hits the heat if the cornstarch will  start to thicken and once you get to  your desired viscosity remove it from  the heat and we're gonna put it into a  bowl so moving on to the pastry part so  today we're going to be using a packaged  puff pastry and if you want to make a  home puff pastry more power to you  because that's actually way more awesome  than getting the packaged stuff but it  does take time to fold the butter into  the flour over and over and over again  to create that puff pastry texture and  I'm opting to do something more simple  and hopefully you guys will appreciate  the simplicity of this so we've already  accomplished all the hard parts which is  just making the filling now it's the fun  part of folding and wrapping the filling  with the puff pastries so we're gonna  take out the puff pastry from the box  since it's folded in thirds and you'll  see the creases right on those creases  we're going to cut it and then from  there we're gonna cut it the other way  around  horizontally so that should give us nine  squares of puff pastry sheets and each  square is going to be its own puff  pastry from there we're going to add one  spoonful a tablespoon of filling on top  of each puff pastry square and once  that's done  the puff pastry square and we're gonna  fold it in half diagonally creating like  a triangle shape and from there what the  trick is is you want to close off the  very top corner and the sides and kind  of work your way towards the middle of  the of each side of the triangle and you  just want to pinch it close to help you  with that having a little bit of water  on hand to close off the edges of the  puff pastry helps a lot to have on hand  some cornstarch or flour as well to re  powder your hand so it doesn't stick  your hands as much either  and so once it's folded up into  triangles we're gonna take our trusty  fork and we're going to take that tips  of the fork and we're gonna press into  the corners of the pastry and what this  does is one creates a really cool  pattern on the outside of the pastry so  it looks extra fancy do it also kind of  I guess seals the edges a little bit  more so that when it bakes it that the  puff pastry does expand it starts the  puff and when it does that it may open  up the pastry you don't want that you  want to keep it as close as possible so  it stays all the moisture stays inside  and you have this beautiful bite of  moist curried beef in the middle right  before it goes into the oven we are  gonna make an egg wash so we're gonna  take 1 egg and 1 tablespoon of water and  an egg wash is something that you're  gonna we're gonna brush on top of the  pastries to give it a nice little glassy  look  and while I we have a batch of nine puff  pastry is ready to go into the oven and  the other should be heated to 400  degrees Fahrenheit and we're gonna place  that into the oven and cook that for 15  minutes into 15 minutes we're gonna have  some awesome curry puff magic going on  in our mouths now make sure you let it  cool off for about 10 minutes after it's  done baking because the middle is gonna  be piping high we don't want to burn  yourselves or burn your tongues thanks  for our awesome afternoon snack  sometimes I even have breakfast goes  really well with a cup of tea or a cup  of matcha also I always end up having an  extra amount of beef curry I always end  up saving that for leftovers for that on  top of rice put it on toast sometimes  and you have these nice little curry  toes open-faced sandwiches it's awesome  it's multi-purpose you do whatever you  want with it and or statement and make  more pastries but like always feel free  to leave a comment down below on what I  should cook next and if you do end up  making this curry puff pastry be sure to  tag me on my instagram when you do make  it I like to check out your creations  and we can compare how perfect our  triangles are when we make these puff  pastries until next time peace  [Music]

